CAT = "cpaiscMitigation"; SEG = "MIT"
Q = [
("Placing payment systems in a separate network zone from office PCs, with traffic between them restricted, is:",
 "Network segmentation", ["Load balancing", "Port mirroring", "Address translation"],
 "Segmentation limits how far an attacker can move and shrinks the scope of systems handling sensitive data. Port mirroring copies traffic for monitoring."),
("Remote staff reach internal systems over the internet through an encrypted tunnel. This is a:",
 "Virtual private network", ["Demilitarized zone", "Reverse proxy server", "Network access control list"],
 "A VPN encrypts traffic between the remote device and the network. A DMZ is a network zone for public-facing servers."),
("A device placed in line with network traffic that blocks attacks, rather than only alerting, is an:",
 "Intrusion prevention system", ["Intrusion detection system", "Log management server", "Packet sniffer"],
 "An IPS can drop malicious traffic, which makes it preventive. An IDS watches and alerts, which makes it detective."),
("Removing unused services, closing open ports and disabling default accounts on a new server is:",
 "System hardening", ["Capacity load testing", "Data masking", "Sandboxing"],
 "Hardening reduces the attack surface by removing what is not needed. Sandboxing runs untrusted code in an isolated space."),
("A company follows defense in depth. An attacker gets past its perimeter firewall. What should the design ensure?",
 "Other layers can still stop the attack", ["The firewall rules are reset at once", "Every user is locked out of the network", "Insurance pays any loss straight away"],
 "Defense in depth layers different controls, such as network, endpoint, access and data controls, so the failure of one layer does not expose the asset. Resetting the firewall or locking everyone out is not what layering provides, and insurance transfers loss rather than stopping an attack."),
("A new accounts payable clerk receives only the system rights needed to enter invoices. This applies:",
 "Least privilege", ["Separation of duties", "Zero trust", "Defense in depth"],
 "Least privilege gives each user the minimum access their job needs. Separation of duties splits incompatible tasks among different people."),
("An analyst who can reach the case database may open only the files for cases assigned to her. This reflects the:",
 "Need-to-know principle", ["Separation of duties", "Mandatory vacation rule", "Dual control rule"],
 "Need to know limits access to the specific information a task requires, even within systems the person can reach. Dual control requires two people for one action."),
("Which security model verifies every access request, even from devices already inside the corporate network?",
 "Zero trust", ["Perimeter security", "Defense in depth", "Implicit trust zones"],
 "Zero trust grants no trust based on network location and checks identity and device for each request. Perimeter security trusts whatever is inside the firewall."),
("Allowing only programs on an approved list to run on company laptops is:",
 "Application whitelisting", ["Application blacklisting", "Sandboxing", "Heuristic antivirus scanning"],
 "Whitelisting (allow listing) blocks everything not approved, including unknown malware. Blacklisting blocks only known bad programs."),
("Vulnerability management is best described as:",
 "Ongoing finding and fixing of flaws", ["A one-time annual penetration test", "Responding to confirmed incidents", "Insuring against breach losses"],
 "Vulnerability management is an ongoing cycle of identifying, rating, remediating and verifying weaknesses. A penetration test is one point-in-time activity within it."),
("A security analyst reviews firewall logs each morning for unusual connections. This is a:",
 "Detective control", ["Preventive control", "Corrective control", "Compensating control"],
 "Log review finds events after they happen, so it is detective. A preventive control, such as a firewall rule, stops the event in the first place."),
("Which of these is a preventive control against cyber-attack?",
 "Device hardening", ["Log analysis", "Intrusion detection", "Restore from backup"],
 "Hardening removes weaknesses before an attack. Log analysis and intrusion detection find attacks, and restoring from backup corrects their effects."),
("A user signs in with a password plus a one-time code from a phone app. This is:",
 "Multi-factor authentication", ["Single sign-on", "Password complexity requirement", "Federated identity"],
 "Combining something you know with something you have is multi-factor authentication. Single sign-on lets one login reach many systems."),
("A fingerprint scan is which type of authentication factor?",
 "Something you are", ["Something you know", "Something you have", "Somewhere you are"],
 "Biometrics measure a physical trait, something you are. A smart card is something you have and a PIN is something you know."),
("Logging in once to reach many separate applications without signing in again is:",
 "Single sign-on", ["Multi-factor authentication", "Role-based access", "Smart card login"],
 "SSO lets one authentication grant access to several systems. It is convenient but makes that one credential more valuable, so it is often paired with MFA."),
("A digital signature mainly assures the recipient of a message's:",
 "Sender and integrity", ["Confidentiality of contents", "Proof of delivery", "Encryption of attachments"],
 "A digital signature, made with the sender's private key, proves who sent the message and that it was not altered. It does not encrypt the contents."),
("Access rights assigned by job function, such as all AP clerks sharing one set of permissions, is which authorization model?",
 "Role-based", ["Discretionary", "Mandatory", "Identity-based"],
 "Role-based access control grants permissions to roles and then assigns users to roles. Discretionary control lets resource owners decide access."),
("Access decided by security labels the system enforces, which users cannot change, is which authorization model?",
 "Mandatory", ["Discretionary", "Role-based", "Attribute-based"],
 "Mandatory access control compares clearance labels with classification labels and does not let owners override them. It is common in military systems."),
("The owner of a spreadsheet decides which colleagues may open and edit it. This is which authorization model?",
 "Discretionary", ["Mandatory", "Role-based", "Rule-based"],
 "Under discretionary access control the resource owner grants access, often through an access control list. Mandatory control removes that choice from owners."),
("A bring-your-own-device policy most often requires employees using personal phones for work to:",
 "Allow remote wipe of work data", ["Install only company-paid apps", "Use the phone only on office Wi-Fi", "Buy the company's chosen phone model"],
 "BYOD policies usually let the company manage or wipe the work container if a device is lost or the person leaves. Limiting the phone to office Wi-Fi would defeat the point of mobile work."),
]
